# 📍 Scope & Lifetime of Variables in Java

> **Scope** defines *where a variable can be accessed*
> **Lifetime** defines *how long a variable exists in memory*

---

# 🧠 Key Idea

* Scope = **visibility of variable**
* Lifetime = **duration of existence**

---

# 🔢 Types of Variables (Based on Scope)

---

## 1. 📍 Local Variables

> Declared inside methods, constructors, or blocks

```java id="vs1"
void display() {
    int x = 10;  // local variable
    System.out.println(x);
}
```

### ✅ Scope

* Only accessible **inside the method/block**

### ⏳ Lifetime

* Created when method starts
* Destroyed when method ends

---

## 2. 🏷️ Instance Variables

> Declared inside class, outside methods

```java id="vs2"
class Student {
    int marks;  // instance variable
}
```

### ✅ Scope

* Accessible throughout the class
* Accessed using **object**

### ⏳ Lifetime

* Exists as long as **object exists**

---

## 3. 🏢 Static Variables (Class Variables)

> Declared using `static` keyword

```java id="vs3"
class Student {
    static String college = "ABC";
}
```

### ✅ Scope

* Shared across all objects
* Access using class name

### ⏳ Lifetime

* Exists from **class loading to program end**

---

# 🧱 Block Scope (Important)

> Variables declared inside `{ }` are limited to that block

```java id="vs4"
if (true) {
    int x = 5;
}
// x is NOT accessible here
```

---

# ⚠️ Important Points

* Local variables must be **initialized before use**
* Instance & static variables get **default values**
* Same variable name can exist in different scopes

---

# 🔄 Example (Scope Understanding)

```java id="vs5"
class Demo {
    int a = 10;  // instance variable

    void show() {
        int b = 5;  // local variable
        System.out.println(a + b);
    }
}
```

